lesson chemistry-changes-you-can-undo v=1 format=teach grade=2 unit=6 subject=chemistry levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Changes you can undo"
  goal "Sort changes into those that can be undone and those that cannot, say how to undo the ones that can, and say why."

  look {
    scene 16x17 {
      beforeafter a change="melt-chocolate" show=1 names=1 at=canvas(1, 0)
      beforeafter b change="toast-bread" show=1 names=1 below=a gap=1
    }
    say """
      Some changes can be undone. Melted chocolate sets hard again in the fridge, and it is still
      chocolate. Other changes make a new material, and cannot be undone. Toast can never be
      turned back into bread.
      """
  }
  do {
    practice chem.change-before count=1 seed=2
    level easy {
      worked chem.change-undo c="dissolve-sugar" level=medium
    }
    practice chem.change-undo count=2 seed=5 hard-seed=1
    practice chem.change-new count=2 seed=1
    practice chem.undo-how count=2 seed=1 easy-seed=2
    practice chem.undo-sort count=2 hard-count=1 seed=4
    level hard {
      practice chem.undo-why count=1 seed=2
    }
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show chem.undo-why c="dissolve-sugar" easy-c="melt-ice"
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show chem.new-gas q=0 hard-q=1
  }
  remember {
    say "If it is the same stuff afterwards, you can change it back. If it is new stuff, you cannot."
  }

  grown-ups """
    Saying what the chocolate or the bread becomes is the way in. The core asks the same idea from
    three sides: can it be undone, is a new material made, and if it can be undone, how. The
    two-star question asks for the reason, from three kinds of change: the same stuff in a new state,
    a solid spread through water, or a new material. The three-star question compares two changes
    that both make a gas: steam is still water, and the gas from vinegar and baking soda was not
    there before. A grown-up does the toasting and the frying; nobody tastes anything but food made
    for eating.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the change to guess is as written, sugar dissolving is judged once in the teacher's pen before ice, water, chocolate, toast, an egg and a cake, which are plain, the way back is cooling or warming, the columns are as written, the two-star asks why melting ice can be undone, and the three-star is as written."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the change to guess is as written, the changes to judge are the subtle ones, sugar dissolving, water boiling, dough rising, a fizz, rust and a candle, the way back is a cooling for steam or the water drying up for sugar, the columns are as written, the two-star is as written and a second reason is asked for another subtle change, and the three-star asks which gas is still water."
  }
}
